ll details. That way, they can verify if the data is structured as expected. \n\nWait, the user already described the ConfigMap and saw that it has the "routing-config" key. But maybe the init container\'s command is expecting a different key or a different format. For example, if the command is trying to access a key that\'s not present, or if the data is in a different format than expected. \n\nAlternatively, the init container might be failing because the command is not correctly parsing the ConfigMap\'s data. For instance, if the command is supposed to extract a specific value but the JSON structure is incorrect. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to check the ConfigMap\'s data in more detail. The user can use kubectl get configmap app-routing-config -n astronomy-shop -o yaml to get the full YAML output, which will show the exact key-value pairs. This will help confirm if the data is present and correctly formatted. \n\nIf the ConfigMap\'s data is correct, then the issue might be with the init container\'s command itself. For example, maybe the command is missing some parameters or the way it\'s processing the ConfigMap\'s data is incorrect. \n\nIn any case, verifying the ConfigMap\'s content is crucial to determine if the init container\'s failure is due to missing or incorrect data. Once that\'s confirmed, the next steps would involve checking the init container\'s command for any errors in how it\'s using the ConfigMap\'s data.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap app-routing-config -n astronomy-shop -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ad526d32b2e9aa6e', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 864, 'prompt_tokens': 6445, 'total_tokens': 7309, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish