gMap, but it\'s crashing with exit code 1. \n\nFirst, the user checked if the ConfigMap exists using kubectl get configmap, and it does exist. Then they described the ConfigMap to check its contents. The output shows that the ConfigMap has one key-value pair: "routing-config" with a value that\'s a JSON object. The init container\'s command is using kubectl to get the ConfigMap and output it as JSON. \n\nWait, the command in the init container is "kubectl get configmap app-routing-config -n astronomy-shop -o json". But the ConfigMap\'s DATA field shows that it has a key "routing-config". So when the init container runs that command, it\'s expecting the ConfigMap to have certain data, but maybe the key is named differently or the data is missing. \n\nThe error message from the init container\'s log was "Error" with exit code 1. That could happen if the ConfigMap doesn\'t have the expected data. For example, if the init container is looking for a specific key that\'s not present, or if the data is malformed. \n\nSo the next step is to check the actual content of the ConfigMap. The user already described the ConfigMap and saw that it has the "routing-config" key. But maybe the init container\'s command is trying to access a different key or the data isn\'t properly formatted. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the init container\'s command is incorrect. The command is using kubectl to get the ConfigMap, but perhaps the init container is supposed to process the ConfigMap\'s data in a different way. For example, if the command is supposed to extract a specific value from the ConfigMap but isn\'t doing so correctly. \n\nAnother possibility is that the ConfigMap is being updated after the init container started, but that\'s less likely. Since the ConfigMap exists and has data, the issue might be with how the init container is using that data. \n\nThe user\'s next step should be to check the exact content of the ConfigMap to ensure it\'s correct. They can do this by running kubectl get configmap app-routing-config -n astronomy-shop -o yaml to see the full details. That way, they can verify if the data is structured as expected. \n\nWait, the user already described the ConfigMap and saw that it has the "routing-config" key. But maybe the init container\'s command is expecting a different key or a different format. For example, if the command is trying to access a key that\'s not present, or if the data is in a different format than expected. \n\nAlternatively, the init container might be failing because the command is not correctly parsing the ConfigMap\'s data. For instance, if the command is supposed to extract a specific value but the JSON structure is incorrect. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to check the ConfigMap\'s data in more detail. The user can use kubectl get configmap app-routing-config -n astronomy-shop -o yaml to get the full YAML output, which will show the exact key-value pairs. This will help confirm if the data is present and correctly formatted. \n\nIf the ConfigMap\'s data is correct, then the issue might be with the init container\'s command itself. For example, maybe the command is missing some parameters or the way it\'s processing the ConfigMap\'s data is incorrect. \n\nIn any case, verifying the ConfigMap\'s content is crucial to determine if the init container\'s failure is due to missing or incorrect data. Once that\'s confirmed, the next steps would involve checking the init container\'s command for any errors in how it\'s using the ConfigMap\'s data.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap app-routing-config -n astronomy-shop -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ad526d32b2e9aa6e', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 864, 'prompt_tokens': 6445, 'total_tokens': 7309, 'completion_tokens_details': None, '