grafana. The user tried to check the role for frontend-rbac-sa but got an error. Now, they need to check if the service account frontend-rbac-sa has the necessary permissions to access the ConfigMap.\n\nSince the previous attempts to check role bindings directly didn\'t work, maybe the service account doesn\'t have a role assigned, or the role doesn\'t have the right permissions. The correct approach is to check the service account\'s role binding explicitly. However, the tool doesn\'t support pipes, so they need to use a command that doesn\'t involve piping.\n\nThe next tool call should be to get the role binding for the frontend-rbac-sa service account. But since the previous attempt to use grep failed, maybe they should just list all role bindings and look for any related to frontend-rbac-sa. Alternatively, check the service account\'s own role bindings directly.\n\nWait, the user tried "kubectl get rolebinding -n astronomy-shop" and got only grafana. Maybe the frontend-rbac-sa doesn\'t have any role bindings, meaning it doesn\'t have the necessary permissions. That would explain why the init container can\'t access the ConfigMap. So the root cause is that the service account lacks the required permissions to read the ConfigMap. Therefore, the solution would be to assign the appropriate role or clusterrole to the service account.\n</think>\n\n**Tool Choice**: `exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd`  \n**Reasoning**: The `frontend-rbac-sa` service account appears to lack explicit role bindings, which are required to access the `app-routing-config` ConfigMap. Without proper permissions, the `config-loader` init container cannot retrieve the ConfigMap, causing the `CrashLoopBackOff` error. To confirm this, I will directly inspect the service account\'s associated role bindings.  \n\n**Tool Call**:  \n```json\n{\n  "name": "exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd",\n  "arguments": {\n    "command": "kubectl get rolebinding -n astronomy-shop | grep frontend-rbac-sa"\n  }\n}\n```', additional